    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 222043\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n143 PM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- The overall pattern will favor dry and tranquil conditions \n  across the Desert Southwest through at least the Thanksgiving \n  Weekend.\n\n- Temperatures over the next couple of days will hover near to \n  slightly below normal before a warming trend pushes readings \n  toward above seasonal levels by the back half of the upcoming \n  week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nBroad troughing continues today over Western CONUS, with H5 \nheights between 568-571 dam over the Desert SW region. These \nheights are near average for this time of year resulting in near \nnormal temperatures expected this afternoon, with much of the \nlower deserts forecasted to be in the lower 70s. By Monday a \nbreak-away piece of energy from the main portion of the trough, \nthat is currently sitting in the easter Pacific, will quickly pass\nover Arizona which will knock temperatures down 1F-2F for Monday.\nAdditionally, over the next couple of days passing high-level \nclouds will continue to be common.\n\nA brief break in this troughing pattern will take place starting \nTuesday as upper level ridging build over the Western CONUS. This \nwill lead to a general warming trend by mid-week, lasting into the \nweekend. This will translate to afternoon temperatures on Wednesday \nbetween 73F-75F for the lower deserts and 50s and 60s remaining in \nplace for the higher terrain. Further warming will be likely as the \nweek goes on.  \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH NEXT SUNDAY/...  \nThe building ridge Tuesday will set the stage for a gradual warming \ntrend through the remainder of the week and into the weekend as anti-\ncyclonic curvature dominates during much of this timeframe. Global \nensembles do show another weak shortwave moving off the eastern \nPacific by Wednesday, likely putting a short pause on the daily \npositive temperature trajectory, but that will quickly be replaced \nwith what is shown to be an unseasonably strong area of high \npressure setting up west of the Baja Peninsula. Afternoon highs by \nThanksgiving will rise far enough to be comfortably above normals \nvalues for the back-end of November, but that will only result in \nmiddle to upper 70s becoming more common across lower elevation \nareas so conditions should still feel quite pleasant. There might be \na few spots, mainly out in SW Arizona and SE California, that reach \ninto the lower 80s by the weekend due to their closer proximity to \nthe greatest height anomalies, but that appears to be the upper \nlimit of how far temperatures will rise. With the high centered to \nour southwest, cool northerly flow will likely be an inhibiting \nfactor as to why we will not see warmth that is completely out of \nthe ordinary for this time of year. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1750Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo weather issues will exist through Monday under FEW to SCT mid\nand high clouds. Confidence is very good that light east winds\nwill be maintained through late afternoon before attempting to\nswitch to westerly around sunset. Extended periods of variable\ndirections or calm conditions will be common. A similar wind\npattern can be expected for Monday.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo weather issues will exist through Monday under high cirrus \ndecks. Extended periods of nearly calm conditions will be common \nwith a north component generally favored at KBLH and a west \ntrajectory at KIPL. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nDry and tranquil conditions will continue across the region with\nMinRH's ranging between 15-30% through most of the forecast\nperiod, and cool overnight lows resulting in decent to good \novernight recovery each night. Temperatures will be near to below\nnormal for the next couple of days before warming up to near to \nslightly above normal by mid-week into the weekend. Outside of \nperiods of marginally breezy conditions over high terrain areas \nand the Lower Colorado River Valley, light winds will be favored \nelsewhere. \n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...Updated 11/22 0910\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area due to continued water releases upwards \nof 3000 cfs. Water levels near Dateland continue to rise, with \ngauge data now showing a depth of around 7 ft and flow approaching\n900 cfs. CBRFC's latest forecast for this station has water \nrising above action stage (8 ft) by late this morning. A separate \nFlood Warning segment remains in effect from Dateland to Yuma. \nThese water levels will have the potential to flood farm fields \nand low water crossings along the river downstream from the dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan \nLONG TERM...RW \nAVIATION...18/Benedict \nFIRE WEATHER...Ryan \nHYDROLOGY...Benedict/RW\n",
